text:
## Why

`jwt.verify()` **throws** on an expired token (`TokenExpiredError`), and nothing catches it.

`src/user/auth.middleware.ts:17`:

```ts
const decoded: any = jwt.verify(token, SECRET);   // ← throws, uncaught
const user = await this.userService.findById(decoded.id);
```

Every other failure path in that file throws `HttpException(..., HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED)`, which Nest maps to a 401. `TokenExpiredError` is not an `HttpException`, and there's no global exception filter registered anywhere in the project (no `useGlobalFilters` / `APP_FILTER` match), so Nest's default handler treats it as an unhandled error → **500 Internal Server Error**. The middleware's own `if (!user)` guard never fires either — execution never reaches it.

This is a known, recorded regression: `LESSONS.md:29` ("An invalid token must be a 401, not a 500", upstream #79) describes exactly this symptom, including the mobile app treating it as an outage instead of logging the user out.

**Second, easily-missed crash site:** `src/user/user.decorator.ts:15` calls `jwt.verify` again, unguarded, for optional auth on *unprotected* routes. So an expired token 500s on public routes too (`GET /articles`, `GET /profiles/:username`, …) — anywhere `@User()` is a handler param. Fixing only the middleware leaves half the bug.

Worth noting on timing: `generateJWT` sets a **60-day** expiry (`user.service.ts:103-114`), so tokens are only now reaching expiry in the field — which is likely why this is surfacing as a new report rather than a day-one bug.

## The fix

**1. `src/user/auth.middleware.ts`** — wrap *only* the `verify` call:

```ts
const token = (authHeaders as string).split(' ')[1];

let decoded: any;
try {
  decoded = jwt.verify(token, SECRET);
} catch (err) {
  throw new HttpException('Not authorized.', HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
}

const user = await this.userService.findById(decoded.id);
req.user = user.user;
next();
```

The scope of the `try` matters. Don't wrap `findById`/`next()` — that would swallow genuine downstream failures (DB errors, handler exceptions) and relabel them as 401s, turning a server outage into a silent forced-logout for every user. Catch the one call that's expected to throw.

**2. `src/user/user.decorator.ts`** — on an unguarded route auth is *optional*, so degrade to anonymous rather than 401:

```ts
if (token && token[1]) {
  try {
    const decoded: any = jwt.verify(token[1], SECRET);
    return !!data ? decoded[data] : decoded.user;
  } catch (err) {
    return undefined;   // bad/expired token on a public route → treat as anonymous
  }
}
```

This matches the existing no-token-at-all behaviour (falls through, returns `undefined`). It's safe for protected routes because those take the `req.user` branch at line 8 — the middleware has already rejected the expired token before the decorator runs.

## Client-side note

The 401 alone won't fix the mobile app if it only distinguishes "network error" from "success". Once this ships, an expired token returns `401 {"message":"Not authorized."}` — the app needs to treat 401 on any authenticated call as *log out and re-auth*, not as a retryable failure. Given the 60-day expiry, this path will now be hit routinely rather than never.

Want me to apply both edits?
